+## Phase 4: The cheat on the page
+
+- [ ] `app.js`, the code: a `keydown` listener on `document` keeps the last
+  10 keys and compares them with
+  `ArrowUp ArrowUp ArrowDown ArrowDown ArrowLeft ArrowRight ArrowLeft ArrowRight b a`,
+  with B and A in either case. It ignores events with Ctrl, Alt or Meta
+  held, `event.repeat`, and keys typed while a `<select>` has the focus. It
+  unlocks only when the mode is `computer` and `state.status` is
+  `playing`, and otherwise does nothing at all. New game, a mode change or a
+  level change locks it again and clears the keys. On unlock, the status
+  line says "Cheat unlocked! Once this game, you may steal one of the
+  computer's squares." The board's own arrow handling does not change.
+- [ ] `app.js`, the panel: unlocking shows the panel and the "With help"
+  column. The column stays visible until the page is reloaded. The button is
+  `aria-disabled="true"` (still focusable) when `state.stealable` is empty.
+  The panel text is then "No O to steal yet" when `steal_used` is false, or
+  "Steal used" when it is true. Otherwise it is "Press Steal, then choose an
+  O. Esc cancels."
+- [ ] `app.js`, steal mode: the button (click, Enter, Space) turns steal
+  mode on only when `stealable` is not empty. It sets `aria-pressed`, says
+  "Steal: choose one of the computer's squares." and moves the focus to the
+  lowest stealable square, using the roving `tabindex`. In steal mode, only
+  the squares in `state.stealable` are `aria-disabled="false"` with class
+  `stealable` and the label suffix ", can be stolen". Choosing one sends
+  `{board, steal, mode, level, game}`. Steal mode ends when the answer
+  arrives, or with Esc or a second press of the button, which puts the
+  focus back on the button. Outside steal mode, an O does nothing, as
+  today.
+- [ ] `app.js`, after a steal: the status line says "You stole row R,
+  column C." when the game goes on, and the usual text comes back on the
+  next render. A win with `assisted: true` shows "You won... with a little
+  help", adds 1 to "With help" and adds nothing to X wins. O wins and draws
+  count as today. `app.js` uses `stealable`, `steal_used` and `assisted`
+  exactly as the server sends them, and never works out a rule itself.
+- [ ] `docs/architecture.md`, "The page": the code and how the page
+  recognises it, locking per game, the panel, steal mode and its keyboard
+  and screen-reader behaviour, and the "With help" score.
+- [ ] Verify: `python3 -m pytest -q` passes, and ruff is clean. A throwaway
+  `python3` check reads the served `app.js`. It must contain the 10-key
+  code, send `steal` and `game`, and read `stealable` and `assisted`. It
+  must hold no rule: no winning-line triples, no counting of X and O, and
+  no working out of which squares can be stolen. Then start
+  `python3 -m tictactoe --port 0` and, with `urllib`, send the exact request
+  body `app.js` builds for a steal: follow a Hard game to an assisted win
+  and an Easy game to a steal that does not win. Also play one tokenless
+  game, to show the old game is unchanged. Stop the server in a `finally`.
+  Everything that only a browser can show goes to the preview.
+
+## For the preview
+
+- Before the code, the page looks and behaves exactly as today: there is no
+  steal button, no hint, no "With help" column, and no change to labels or
+  status texts. A screen reader announces nothing new.
+- Before the code, clicking, tapping, or pressing Enter or Space on an O
+  does nothing.
+- Typing ↑ ↑ ↓ ↓ ← → ← → B A during a game against the computer (Easy and
+  Hard) unlocks the steal: the panel and the "With help" column appear, and
+  "Cheat unlocked! …" is announced.
+- Typing it with uppercase B A works. Extra keys before the code still
+  work, for example ↑ ↑ ↑ ↓ ↓ …. A wrong key in the middle does not unlock.
+  Keys held down (auto-repeat) or typed with Ctrl, Alt or Meta are ignored.
+- Typing the code with the focus on the board only moves the focus around
+  and never plays a move. Typing it with the focus on a `<select>` does not
+  unlock.
+- In friend mode, and after a game has ended, the code does nothing: no
+  message and no hint.
+- Before the computer's first move, the button looks disabled and the panel
+  says "No O to steal yet". After a steal, it says "Steal used" and the
+  button stays disabled-looking.
+- Steal with the mouse: press Steal, and only the O squares get the dashed
+  outline. Clicking an empty square or an X does nothing. Clicking an O
+  turns it into an X, and the computer replies.
+- Steal with the keyboard alone: Enter or Space on the button moves the
+  focus to the lowest stealable O. The arrow keys move the focus, Enter
+  steals, and Esc (or pressing the button again) cancels and puts the focus
+  back on the button.
+- A screen reader reads ", can be stolen" on stealable squares, the button's
+  pressed state, and the status messages ("Steal: choose…", "You stole row
+  R, column C.").
+- A win that used a steal shows "You won... with a little help". "With
+  help" goes up by 1 and "X wins" does not. A computer win or a draw after a
+  steal counts as today.
+- A new game, a mode change or a level change hides the steal again until
+  the code is typed again. The "With help" column stays until reload.
+- At 320 px wide, in light and dark mode, the panel and the four-column
+  score fit with no horizontal scroll, the button is at least 44 px tall,
+  and the dashed outline is clear without relying on colour.
+- A whole game without the code, in each mode and level, plays exactly as
+  before.
